usage: latte <command> [options]

commands:
  init <name>    write a project: a layout, a page, and the manifests
  build          regenerate the markup, then compile
  check          regenerate and type-check; nothing is compiled
  generate       compile the markup and stop
  clean          remove the build directory
  vocabulary     print latte's .bx surface as JSON, for an editor
  version        print the version and stop

options:
  -c, --configuration <debug|release>   which profile (default: debug)
      --release / --debug               the same two, spelled the short way
      --target <html|canvas>            (init) which target; default html
      --latte <path>                    (init) build against a latte checkout
      --here                            (init) write into this directory
      --drift                           (check) fail on a stale generated file
      --generated                       (clean) remove generated/ as well

An application does not need latte to build: generated/ is checked in, so a
clone compiles with plain beansc and no latte binary present at all. This is
what you need to write one.
